==German==
===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|stan|stān|lang=goh}}, {{term|sten|stēn|lang=goh}}, from {{proto|Germanic|stānan|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃteːən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-stehen.ogg|Audio}}
* {{audio|De-at-stehen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-irregular|steht|stand|gestanden|past subjunctive=[[stände]] or [[stünde]]}}

# {{intransitive|lang=de}} to [[stand]] (to be upright, support oneself on the feet in an erect position) 
# {{intransitive|lang=de}} to [[be]], to [[stand]] (to be placed or located somewhere)
#* '''1931''', [[w:Arthur Schnitzler|Arthur Schnitzler]], ''[[s:de:Flucht in die Finsternis|Flucht in die Finsternis]]'', [[w:S. Fischer Verlag|S. Fischer Verlag]], page 36:
#*:Ein frisch gefülltes Glas Champagner '''stand''' vor ihm. Er trank es in einem Zug aus – mit Lust, fast mit Begier.
#*::A freshly filled glass of champaign was in front of him. He emptied it in one draught – with pleasure, almost with greed.
# {{intransitive|lang=de}} to [[stay]]; to be [[still]]

====Usage notes====
In all Austrian and Southern German dialects as well as standard language, the auxiliary verb {{term|sein}} is used instead of {{term|haben}} when forming the composite tenses.

